# ============================================================================
# Homelab custom Suricata rules — C2 / adversary emulation (Sliver).
# Detection-as-code companion to the Wazuh local_rules.xml. Loaded via the
# `rule-files:` list in suricata.yaml; sids in the 9100000+ lab-local range.
#
# Context: a Sliver HTTPS *beacon* on a CORP host (fs-01, 10.10.10.20) calling
# home to the attacker box atk-01 (REDTEAM, 10.10.40.119:443). The C2 is TLS 1.3,
# so the certificate is encrypted in the handshake (Suricata logs tls.subject =
# None) and ET Open's cert/content signatures never fire. These two rules close
# that gap from different angles — a server-side TLS fingerprint and the
# beaconing behavior itself. Full writeup: phase-5-offense/sliver-c2/.
# ============================================================================

# (1) Sliver / Go C2 over HTTPS — implant TLS fingerprint (JA3, client-side).
# The Sliver beacon's Go crypto/tls ClientHello has a stable JA3
# (725543c78edf669194c11dc7a039b56e). Deliberately matched on the CLIENT ja3,
# not the server ja3s: JA3S lives in the ServerHello (to_client), and a
# `-> dest 443` rule is evaluated to_server, so a ja3s.hash rule here silently
# never fires — the ClientHello ja3 is on the to_server side and matches. (The
# server ja3s is f4febc55ea12b31ae17cfb7e614afda8 if a to_client rule is ever
# wanted.) Scoped to REDTEAM:443 — a CORP host speaking Go-TLS to the attacker
# segment is the anomaly. NB: Go-TLS JA3 is shared by other Go clients, so it is
# paired with the destination scope and the beaconing rule (2), not trusted alone.
alert tls $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.40.0/24 443 (msg:"LAB SLIVER C2 HTTPS beacon - Go-TLS JA3 implant fp to REDTEAM (T1071.001)"; flow:established,to_server; ja3.hash; content:"725543c78edf669194c11dc7a039b56e"; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9100001; rev:2; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0011, mitre_technique T1071_001;)

# (2) Beaconing behavior — repeated short-lived connections from one internal
# host to a REDTEAM host on 443. Encryption-agnostic: it catches the *pattern*
# (regular automated check-ins) even if the TLS fingerprint rotates. At the
# beacon's ~5s interval a source produces >10 SYNs/min, so 10-in-60s fires while
# ordinary browsing (which reuses connections) does not.
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.40.0/24 443 (msg:"LAB C2 beaconing - repeated conns to REDTEAM:443 (T1071)"; flags:S; detection_filter:track by_src, count 10, seconds 60; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:9100002; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0011, mitre_technique T1071;)

# ============================================================================
# DNS tunneling / exfiltration over DNS (T1071.004 Application Layer Protocol:
# DNS; T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol). An implant encodes
# data into the subdomain labels of an attacker-controlled zone; outbound DNS is
# almost always permitted, so it slips egress filtering. Verified against an
# iodine tunnel from fs-01 (CORP) to atk-01 (REDTEAM) — Zeek/Suricata logged
# query names 590-1000+ chars long (normal names are <40). Full writeup +
# quantitative dns.log hunt: phase-6-nsm/dns-tunneling.md.
# ============================================================================

# (3) DNS tunneling — abnormally long, high-entropy query names to REDTEAM, at
# volume. The length alone is a strong signal (a 100+ char qname is already
# abnormal; a tunnel's are far longer); the detection_filter requires 20 such
# names/min from one source, so a rare long lookup doesn't alert but a tunnel's
# sustained stream does. Encoding-agnostic (matches the length/shape, not a
# specific codec). Scoped to REDTEAM:53 — a CORP host streaming long DNS names to
# the attacker segment is the anomaly.
alert dns $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.40.0/24 53 (msg:"LAB DNS tunneling - long encoded query names to REDTEAM (T1071.004 / T1048.003)"; dns.query; pcre:"/^.{100}/"; detection_filter:track by_src, count 20, seconds 60; classtype:data-theft; sid:9100010; rev:2; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0010, mitre_technique T1071_004, mitre_technique T1048_003;)

# ============================================================================
# DMZ web-attack detection — T1190 Exploit Public-Facing Application. The DMZ
# runs OWASP Juice Shop on 10.10.20.10:3000. Two reasons these lab rules exist
# rather than leaning on ET Open alone:
#   (1) ET Open's web-attack signatures match on $HTTP_PORTS, which by default is
#       80 (+443/8080) and does NOT include 3000 — so out of the box the entire
#       ET web ruleset is BLIND to Juice Shop. The router role adds 3000 to
#       HTTP_PORTS to fix that at the source; these rules are the lab-labeled,
#       MITRE-mapped complement scoped to the DMZ app.
#   (2) They stamp the attack as T1190 with a lab-local sid, so the SIEM shows a
#       labeled web-attack alert (Wazuh child rule 100440 on 86601), not an
#       anonymous ET hit. Suricata parses HTTP on 3000 via protocol detection
#       (the intra-segment traffic is plain HTTP), so http.* sticky buffers work.
# Scoped by DESTINATION (dmz-01:3000) since atk-01's REDTEAM address is DHCP.
# Exercised with sqlmap / nikto / curl probes from atk-01 — see
# phase-5-offense/attack-detect-writeups/03-web-attack-juiceshop-t1190.md.
# ============================================================================

# (20) SQL injection in the request URI — tautology / UNION / stacked-query /
# time-based patterns. Hits Juice Shop's product-search sink (/rest/products/search?q=).
alert http $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.20.10 3000 (msg:"LAB WEB ATTACK SQLi in URI to DMZ Juice Shop (T1190)"; flow:established,to_server; http.uri; pcre:"/(union\s+select|information_schema|\bor\b\s+1\s*=\s*1|'\s*or\s*'?1|sleep\s*\(|benchmark\s*\(|waitfor\s+delay|;\s*drop\s+table)/i"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9100020; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0001, mitre_technique T1190;)

# (21) SQL injection in the POST body — Juice Shop's classic login bypass posts
# {"email":"' OR 1=1--","password":"x"} to /rest/user/login.
alert http $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.20.10 3000 (msg:"LAB WEB ATTACK SQLi in POST body to DMZ Juice Shop (T1190)"; flow:established,to_server; http.request_body; pcre:"/(union\s+select|\bor\b\s+1\s*=\s*1|'\s*or\s*'1'\s*=\s*'1|'\s*--|sleep\s*\()/i"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9100021; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0001, mitre_technique T1190;)

# (22) Cross-site scripting — reflected script/handler payloads in the URI (Juice
# Shop's search field reflects into the DOM).
alert http $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.20.10 3000 (msg:"LAB WEB ATTACK XSS in URI to DMZ Juice Shop (T1190)"; flow:established,to_server; http.uri; pcre:"/(<script|%3cscript|onerror\s*=|onload\s*=|javascript:|<img[^>]+src\s*=)/i"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9100022; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0001, mitre_technique T1190;)

# (23) Path traversal / local file inclusion — ../ sequences and canonical
# sensitive-file targets (Juice Shop exposes an /ftp directory-traversal sink).
alert http $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.20.10 3000 (msg:"LAB WEB ATTACK path traversal / LFI to DMZ Juice Shop (T1190)"; flow:established,to_server; http.uri; pcre:"/(\.\.\/|\.\.%2f|%2e%2e%2f|%2e%2e\/|\/etc\/passwd|\/proc\/self\/)/i"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9100023; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0001, mitre_technique T1190;)

# (24) Web scanner / automated tooling by User-Agent — sqlmap, nikto, gobuster,
# ffuf, nuclei etc. Tagged Reconnaissance (T1595.002 Active Scanning) — the tell
# that an automated web attack is under way, not manual browsing.
alert http $HOME_NET any -> 10.10.20.10 3000 (msg:"LAB WEB ATTACK automated scanner User-Agent against DMZ Juice Shop (T1595.002)"; flow:established,to_server; http.user_agent; pcre:"/(sqlmap|nikto|gobuster|ffuf|dirbuster|nuclei|wpscan|masscan|acunetix|nmap\s+scripting)/i"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:9100024; rev:1; metadata:mitre_tactic TA0043, mitre_technique T1595_002;)
